WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:14.740
Identity and access management, or IAM, is the set of controls that lets an application identify users, services, devices, workloads, and automation, then decide what those identities are allowed to do.

2
00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:19.960
For developers, IAM is not just an infrastructure setting or a login screen.

3
00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:22.540
It is part of the application behavior.

4
00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:31.840
Every protected route, API call, tenant boundary, administrative action, token exchange, and audit event depends on IAM decisions being implemented correctly.

5
00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:35.160
IAM combines several related ideas.

6
00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:37.340
Authentication verifies identity.

7
00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:42.080
Authorization decides whether that identity can perform a particular action.

8
00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:48.340
Identity lifecycle covers creation, changes, suspension, deletion, and recovery.

9
00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:53.000
Sessions and tokens preserve identity and permission state across requests.

10
00:00:53.100 --> 00:01:01.100
Auditability records security-relevant actions so teams can investigate, prove control operation, and understand misuse.

11
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:07.120
When any of these pieces are weak, the application can fail even if the rest of the code looks well built.

12
00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:10.920
The identity provider and the application share responsibility.

13
00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:19.100
A mature provider may handle password storage, MFA, federation, single sign-on, token issuance, and recovery workflows.

14
00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:32.240
The application still has to validate tokens correctly, enforce authorization server-side, map claims to local permissions safely, protect sessions, handle account lifecycle changes, and log important decisions.

15
00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:43.540
IAM mistakes often become application security failures because the code trusts the wrong signal, enforces checks in the wrong place, or assumes identity automatically implies permission.
